592186065201 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065239 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065239 #edb/ref 1061 4284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065239 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "89060a03-576a-52b0-8345-c548845df17b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065239 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065240 #edb/ref 1061 4291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5eab9871-4247-51fe-85ce-5a836f6d3bc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065343 #edb/ref 1064 "When simplifying an algebraic expression involving square roots, it's useful to remember that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|.\n$$\n\nIt's tempting to think that $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is equivalent to $x,$ but that's not true because $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is always non-negative, even if $x$ is negative.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate $\\sqrt{x^2}$ for a couple of different values of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16}=4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn both cases, using absolute value gives the correct result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}}=|4| & =4 & ✓ \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}}=|-4| & =4 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065343 #edb/ref 1065 1604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065343 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4e09a82d-1598-56ed-9888-9a1bfa380f55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065343 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
